## How to build numeric models that beat Kaggle data science winners

Welcome to the chapter on building individual numeric models. This is the first of two parts to analyze numeric data, the second (next) chapter will build on the foundation of this chapter and show how to build ensembles of numeric models. In many ways, this is an excellent place to start. Everyone is familiar with numeric data, and this pair of chapters will show you how to get the most accurate results from virtually any numeric data set.

Once you are comfortable, analyzing numeric data, you will be able to work with data that predicts all of these types of results and many more:

-   Sale price of houses

-   Mileage (miles per quantity of fuel) for vehicles

-   Food quality ratings (if they are numeric)

-   Student performance

-   Energy Efficiency

-   Strength of Concrete

-   Parkinson's Telemonitoring

-   Soybean yield

Many other types of data will also work with the methods we will learn in these two chapters. You will be equipped to do an enormous amount of good with the skills you learn in these two chapters analyzing numeric data sets. This promises to be a lot of fun and very practical, so let's get started!

## Summary of what is coming

This chapter will show how to build individual numeric models of housing prices, including the following components:

-   A Kaggle data science competition to use as a baseline. Our models will beat the best score in the competition.

-   How to read in a data set

-   How to set up the data set so the analysis are much easier to perform

-   How to randomize the data before you analyze it so the results generalize more accurately

-   How to set up the data to be randomly resampled

-   How to set up optimized (tuned) models

-   How to set up summary plots

-   How to set up summary reports

-   How to build an intermediate level solution (uses numeric data and strings)

-   How to build an advanced solution (working with numeric data, strings, factors, and a stratified column to model prices of BMW cars)

-   Actual code you may use or adapt for your own projects and keep in your code library for future use

## A Kaggle data science competition to use as a baseline for the first example

Our very first example is one I am looking forward to sharing with you, but not for all the reasons you might expect. We will be looking at the famous Boston housing data set. The data set was originally created in 1970 and used in an article published in 1978. Compared to almost anything else you will ever work with in your entire career, this data is ancient!

A huge advantage of using the Boston Housing data set is that it has been used by a large number of previous investigators, so it serves as a benchmark for the accuracy of algorithms.

The data also has a substantial number of problems which we are going to work to identify, and that's why I'm interested in sharing it with you. The data set has been criticized, correctly, for several issues. As we build our library of solutions, we want to test if these issues show up. Assuming they show up for the Boston housing data set (and the issues will show up!), it is a reasonable conclusion that similar issues will also show up for other data sets, and in my experience, this is very true.

**Better than AI Solution:** Every AI system is trained on lots of data, and designed to predict the most likely outcome. Our solutions will return great results (of course), but we will use human intelligence to see things no AI system is going to return to you. One of those will be training you to use your judgement when there are problems in the data. The problems can be how the data was recorded, poor ethical choices by prior researchers, clear examples of discrimination, and much more.

Once you learn how to see these types of problems, it will be easier to find them in other data sets.

One of the areas that can set you apart as a data scientist is being able to identify these kinds of issues, and the Boston housing data set is a great example of this exact type of solution. Once you see how this works, feel free to use it on other data sets and include those results in your portfolio.

::: {.callout-note appearance="minimal"}
"In a world of AI, your freedom is in your unpredictability. You have a whole host of large language models all of which are trained to do the most predictable thing next. One of the things that differentiates humans from AI (amongst many) is that we're not the most efficient of beings. The things that are the most meaningful to us are not necessarily the things that are the most predictable to us. "

"As a harmonist, it was one of my greatest joys to take a song or a melody and find kind of the most unexpected things to do with it. It would be like whatever you think I'm gong to do, I'm going to do the opposite of that"

" The right amount of irreverence can be so liberating."\
\
Jacob Collier, <https://youtube.com/shorts/QnGXDfi_itI?si=NM2ispZEpwcUuNUX>
:::

::: callout-note
"AI will not solve poverty, because the conditions that lead to societies that pursue profit over people are not technical. AI will not solve discrimination... the cultural patterns... are not technical."

— Joy Buolamwini (Computer Scientist & Digital Activist), *Unmasking AI: My Mission to Protect What Is Human in a World of Machines*, 2023, p. xix
:::

**First** Let's start by becoming familiar with the Boston housing data set.

You may either download the data from the Kaggle.com website. Here are the first ten rows of the data (total of 506 rows in the original data):

|    crim |   zn | indus | chas |    nox |    rm |  age |    dis | rad | tax | ptratio |  black | lstat | medv |
